Leave-one-out cross-validation ranks the three fits:

| Model | elpd_loo difference | SE of difference | Parameters |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Partial pooling | 0.0 | — | 7 |
| No pooling | −1.8 | 2.4 | 6 |
| Complete pooling | −214.6 | 21.3 | 2 |

Complete pooling is decisively worse and the gap is many times its standard error, which
is the quantitative form of the mis-specification its predictive check showed.

Partial and no pooling are statistically indistinguishable on elpd — a difference of
$1.8$ against a standard error of $2.4$ is no difference at all. That result is easy to
misread as "the hierarchy bought nothing", and it is worth being precise about why it
did not:

- **Predictive accuracy is not the reason to pool.** With five groups and four of them
  well-observed, the two models make nearly identical predictions for 96% of the data.
  LOO weights every observation equally, so the 90 observations where they disagree
  cannot move the total much.
- **The interval width is the reason to pool.** The partial-pooling posterior for
  `ap-south` is 38% narrower than the no-pooling one, and it is narrower without being
  wrong: it still covers the generator's true value for that region.

The honest summary is that partial pooling costs nothing and buys a usable estimate for
the group that needed one, which is the trade this comparison was set up to expose.
